{
 "lines": [
  {
   "id": "VO-01",
   "start": 0.16,
   "end": 5.38,
   "text": "Same thousand dollars, same S and P five hundred, one friend ended with ten times as much."
  },
  {
   "id": "VO-02",
   "start": 5.72,
   "end": 7.1,
   "text": "Wait, how is that possible?"
  },
  {
   "id": "VO-03",
   "start": 7.38,
   "end": 14.6,
   "text": "They all bought in at the start of two thousand six, then the two thousand eight crash cut each thousand to about seven hundred seventy."
  },
  {
   "id": "VO-04",
   "start": 14.94,
   "end": 17.86,
   "text": "Jay sells at year-end and hides the cash in a shoebox."
  },
  {
   "id": "VO-05",
   "start": 18.48,
   "end": 23.52,
   "text": "Maya sells too, then buys back a year later, after missing a twenty-six percent rebound."
  },
  {
   "id": "VO-06",
   "start": 24.06,
   "end": 27.0,
   "text": "Sam stays invested and lets his dividends reinvest."
  },
  {
   "id": "VO-07",
   "start": 27.46,
   "end": 31.2,
   "text": "At first, Jay feels smart, because shoebox money can't crash."
  },
  {
   "id": "VO-08",
   "start": 31.64,
   "end": 39.14,
   "text": "By the end of twenty twenty-five, Jay has seven hundred seventy, Maya about sixty-four hundred, and Sam about eight thousand."
  },
  {
   "id": "VO-09",
   "start": 39.76,
   "end": 46.16,
   "text": "Selling in a crash is the shoebox mistake, and coming back just one year late cost Maya about seventeen hundred."
  },
  {
   "id": "VO-10",
   "start": 46.94,
   "end": 50.98,
   "text": "So if your money crashed tomorrow, would you sell, wait or hold?"
  }
